sn22.84:10.2 | S: And this is what it means. | T: This here is the meaning:
sn22.84:10.3 | S: ‘A person who is not skilled in the path’ is a term for an ordinary unlearned person. | T: ‘The man unskilled in the path’: this is a designation for the worldling.
sn22.84:10.4 | S: ‘A person who is skilled in the path’ is a term for the Realized One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha. | T: ‘The man skilled in the path’: this is a designation for the Tathagata, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One.
sn22.84:10.5 | S: ‘A fork in the road’ is a term for doubt. | T: ‘The forked road’: this is a designation for doubt.
sn22.84:10.6 | S: ‘The left-hand path’ is a term for the wrong eightfold path, that is, | T: ‘The left-hand branch’: this is a designation for the wrong eightfold path; that is,
sn22.84:10.7 | S: wrong view … wrong immersion. | T: wrong view … wrong concentration.
sn22.84:10.8 | S: ‘The right-hand path’ is a term for the noble eightfold path, that is, | T: ‘The right-hand branch’: this is a designation for the Noble Eightfold Path; that is,
sn22.84:10.9 | S: right view … right immersion. | T: right view … right concentration.
sn22.84:10.10 | S: ‘A dense forest grove’ is a term for ignorance. | T: ‘The dense thicket’: this is a designation for ignorance.
sn22.84:10.11 | S: ‘An expanse of low-lying marshes’ is a term for sensual pleasures. | T: ‘The vast marshy swamp’: this is a designation for sensual pleasures.
sn22.84:10.12 | S: ‘A large, steep cliff’ is a term for anger and distress. | T: ‘The steep precipice’: this is a designation for despair due to anger.
sn22.84:10.13 | S: ‘Level, cleared parkland’ is a term for extinguishment. | T: ‘The delightful expanse of level ground’: this is a designation for Nibbāna.
sn22.84:10.14 | S: Rejoice, Tissa, rejoice! | T: “Rejoice, Tissa! Rejoice, Tissa!
sn22.84:10.15 | S: I am here with advice, with support, with instruction.” | T: I am here to exhort, I am here to assist, I am here to instruct!”
sn22.84:11.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn22.84:11.2 | S: Satisfied, Venerable Tissa approved what the Buddha said. | T: Elated, the Venerable Tissa delighted in the Blessed One’s statement.
sn22.84:11.3 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn16.10
sn16.10:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn16.10:1.2 | S: At one time Venerable Mahākassapa was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Mahakassapa was dwelling at Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park.
sn16.10:1.3 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, went to Mahākassapa and said, | T: Then, in the morning, the Venerable Ānanda dressed and, taking bowl and robe, he approached the Venerable Mahakassapa and said:
sn16.10:1.4 | S: “Come, Honorable Kassapa. Let’s go to one of the nuns’ quarters.” | T: “Come, Venerable Kassapa, let us go to the bhikkhunīs’ quarters.”
sn16.10:1.5 | S: “You go, Reverend Ānanda. You’re the one with many duties and responsibilities.” | T: “You go, friend Ānanda, you’re the busy one with many duties.”
sn16.10:1.6 | S: And a second time … | T: A second time the Venerable Ānanda said to the Venerable Mahakassapa:
sn16.10:1.7 | P: “āyāma, bhante kassapa, yena aññataro bhikkhunupassayo tenupasaṅkamissāmā”ti. | T: “Come, Venerable Kassapa, let us go to the bhikkhunīs’ quarters.”

